From: Harvey Newstrom <hnewstrom@hnewstrom.ess.harris.com>
Subject: Deprenyl Comments

Mike Price asks a very good question:
>A question specifically for Harvey - is your identical twin on a similar
>life-extension program and does he look the same age as you?

I have been taking vitamins, amino-acids, antioxidants, and smart drugs for 
over 10 years.  I also have been vegetarian for 8 years.  My brother does 
not take any nutrients or smart drugs, and is not a vegetarian.  We are 
both 30 years old.

Ten years ago most people could not tell my brother and I apart.  Now, we 
do not look very similar at all.  Some people even question if we are 
"identical"!  I am not sure that he looks "older" than me, but other people 
confirm that I look healthier and sexier.  His skin is not as smooth, but 
neither of us actually have noticeable wrinkles.  My skin color is more 
even all over my body.  We weight the same, but I have more muscle/less fat 
than he does.  I also have better energy and stamina, and am more 
productive at work or at play.  I also used to be the "dumb" one, more 
interested in partying and sex than studying when we were younger, but now 
I am the "smart" one with the successful job, published book, interesting 
projects in the works, and a lot of interesting research areas I am 
pursuing.  My brother is a typical computer nerd.  He tells me that he is 
the best programmer in the world, and can make a computer do anything he 
wants, he just can't think of any projects to do.  He also is not 
interested in any of my projects which I will gladly let him work on.  He 
and I have grown apart in philosophy and attitudes as well.

Two people in particular have discussed this subject with me.  They both 
claim that I am the one that has changed most in appearance, and have grown 
intellectually, socially, and emotionally.  Everyone agrees that I have 
changed for the better.  One person is my lover who finds me extremely 
attractive, but does not find my twin attractive at all.  Other people 
confirm that even though my brother and I look similar (same genes), I am 
attractive and healthy looking, while my brother is not very healthy 
looking.  The other person to comment was a very close friend who believes 
that I have greatly improved my appearance since she has known me.  She 
does not care about nutrients, and ascribes my changes to taking control of 
my life and thinking positive.  I made specific goals and plans for my life 
and have worked to acheive them.  My brother goes day to day trying to 
figure out what he wants to be when he grows up.

I do know we have grown physically apart.  I think the most likely 
explanation is the chemical effects of nutrients, anti-aging drugs, and 
good diet.  A secondary set of contributing factors include my dynamic 
optimism along with better goal setting and planning.  I really believe 
that happiness, a good love/sex life, and self-respect contribute to good 
health and a likelihood to succeed.

One interesting footnote:  I once had an argument with my brother.  I 
argued that it was possible to increase one's strength, intelligence and 
health by life-extension and other techniques.  My brother argued that this 
was impossible and that each person was stuck with the cards life dealt 
them.
