From: Harvey Newstrom <hnewstrom@hnewstrom.ess.harris.com>
Subject: Intermittent supplement use?

Nancy bovitz writes:
>I've been thinking about using supplements, but I've had a few times
>in the past when I've had real trouble swallowing pills--I'd throw
>up when I tried, even the Twinlab Daily One capsules which are
>close to flavorless. (I'm not sure of the reason.) I don't know whether 
>this will ever happen again, but is it better to not use supplements than 
>to stop for a couple of weeks? 
>
>I know that you should taper off of high doses of vitamin C--are there any 
>others for which a sudden stop is a bad idea? 

Most nutrients need to be tapered-off to prevent side-effects, especially 
at higher doses.  If you have problems (like above), however, I would 
discontinue immediately.  Any definite side-effects would be worse than the 
subtle effects of not tapering off.

If you have a reaction within a minute of swallowing the pills, I would say 
a physical irritation of the pills in the stomach was occurring.  If you 
have a reaction five minutes later, I would guess that you are reacting to 
the substance in the pills.  You could try another brand as you may be 
reacting to the fillers used to make the pills.

I am not familiar with Twinlab's Daily One pills, but many vitamin pills 
are acidic.  They combine all the acidic vitamins without any pH buffering.  
Do you have heartburn or a warmish pain in your stomach before you throw 
up?

Disclaimer:  This is not a disclaimer.  I am not a doctor.  This is not 
medical advice.  The government is your friend.  Nothing can go wrong...
