competitors, including nCube, Floating Point Systems, Ametek, and
Thinking Machines were lining up at the trough, and Intel would be
unable to sell machines unless they were partially subsidized by the
Feds the way the competing machines were.

(The way it worked is that DARPA subsidized several universities in
acquiring computers, in conjunction with the High Performance
Computing Initiative or somesuch, and thus some sites got
supercomputers at a deep discount. Intel played the game and thus got
the Caltech installation for a "Paragon" hypercube supercomputer, one
